  "file": "test/leases.test.ts",
  "A": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "A developer mistakenly reconstructs an old callback using the ownership identity after recovery advances its generation. The existing recovery tests still pass, leaving that mistake undetected.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "bun test \"$TMPDIR/f1-adjudication-da0p1wbc/mutant.test.ts\" --test-name-pattern 'recover|construction|renewal discovers'",
    "output": "In temporary copies with imports redirected to HEAD, the original tests and a mutation replacing all four `f.bind(manager, expected)` calls with `f.bind(manager, f.capture())` both returned: 10 pass, 44 filtered out, 0 fail, 501 expect() calls. Subsequently, the later commit's new test passed unchanged; replacing its stale binding's expected identity with f.capture() failed: Expected constructor: ClaimWaitError; Received function did not throw."
   },
   "evidence": "HEAD captures before construction using `const expected = f.capture(); const manager = await f.create(); const claim = f.bind(manager, expected);`. However, capture selects `row.confirmed?.generation ?? row.intent?.record.generation ?? 0`, and the existing fixtures preserve the captured generation through recovery. The corresponding base fixtures already exercised recovery but lacked these new bindings and guard assertions. The request explicitly requires a case that fails when identities are derived only after recovery; HEAD's tests do not detect that mutation. This establishes a test requirement gap, not a demonstrated production defect. Only after completing this HEAD assessment, I inspected the response commit: it adds a lost-generation-1/pending-generation-2 acquisition fixture, verifies recovery advances ownership, renews generation 2, and checks that the reconstructed generation-1 callback cannot publish."
  },
  "B": {
   "verdict": "REAL",
   "failure_scenario": "Suppose a later change records a callback's lease identity after the lease manager finishes startup recovery instead of before it. Every recovery test still passes, so nothing catches the regression that issue test 12 explicitly requires to fail. That gap matters when recovery moves the confirmed generation forward: an old callback could then end up bound to the newer generation.",
   "reproduction": {
    "command": "T=$(mktemp -d \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/f1.XXXX\"); rsync -a --exclude node_modules --exclude .git ./ $T/; ln -s \"$PWD/node_modules\" $T/node_modules; cd $T; python3 script that rewrites test/leases.test.ts: 'const expected = f.capture();\\n    const manager = await f.create();' becomes 'const manager = await f.create();\\n    const expected = f.capture();' (2 sites, the :931 and :978 fixtures), and 'const claim = f.bind(manager, expected);' after create in the :1091 fixture becomes 'f.bind(manager, f.capture())' (1 site); bun test test/leases.test.ts",
    "output": "replaced 3\n 54 pass\n 0 fail\n 4178 expect() calls\nRan 54 tests across 1 file. (A run filtered with -t \"recover|captured\" gave 9 pass, 0 fail.)"
   },
   "evidence": "At head, every recovery fixture captures the identity before construction (test/leases.test.ts:931 `const expected = f.capture(); const manager = await f.create(); const claim = f.bind(manager, expected);`, and the same at :978, :502 and :1112). In each of them recovery leaves the generation unchanged: the :931 fixture asserts `confirmed: { generation: 1 ... }` after recovery, and the release/superseded/pending cases keep generation 1. So capturing before or after construction yields the same handle. The issue's test 12 lists 'derive identities only after recovery' as a mutation the tests must catch, and with the mutation applied the whole file passed (54 pass, 0 fail). The source code itself is not shown to be wrong: the claim is only about missing mutation coverage."
  }
 }
